$ cat about.txt | fold -w 75

    a minimal reverse-mode autograd engine in c, just big enough to train
    karpathy's microGPT. float64 tensors, a tape walked in reverse, and 12
    ops with forward and backward next to each other.

    correctness by:

    - numeric parity with karpathy's microGPT to within 1e-11 atol
    - a finite-difference gradient check for every op and the full model
    - asan and ubsan on every test run

    design decisions:

    - 2-D float64 only. no strides, views or broadcasting. float64 because
      the reference uses python floats.
    - a tape instead of refcounted graph nodes. creation order is already a
      topological order, so backward is one reverse loop.
    - static allocation in the style of tigerbeetle. the tape (128 nodes) and
      one 256 KiB arena for op outputs, grads and scratch are fixed at
      compile time. nothing is allocated after startup. tape_reset rewinds
      two offsets.
    - backward kernels are plain loops that never call ops, so backward
      cannot grow the graph it is walking.
    - attention is composed from primitive ops, not fused.
    - asserts are the only error handling and stay on in release builds.
    - not adopted from tigerbeetle and nasa's power of ten: 2 asserts per
      function and no function pointers. both would make the code longer
      and split each op's forward from its backward.

    discussion on hackernews: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=46285424

$ make parity

    trained 1000 steps in 0.101s
    passed parity check (max |diff| 5.7e-15)
    inference (random sampled name): kaonle

see also:

- counterpart in lean: https://github.com/sueszli/autograd.lean
- original microgpt implementation: https://karpathy.github.io/2026/02/12/microgpt/
